h of Fidel Castro  into a pat on the back for Obama you can  disagree with the moves that President  Obama has made up but still recognize  that President Obama was hugely uh  involved in some of these changes that  we've seen and the identity of Cuba uh  is something that has been in extra  kabli tied up in the identity of the  dell castro for a very long time his  passing even though we've seen Cuba  start to move on economically in many  ways may allow Cuba to move on  ideologically culturally in a way that  was only allowed legally by the changes  to the US relationship with Cuba that  we've seen under President Obama  although maybe not ideologically and  culturally as much and there's something  sort of I don't know if the word is  bittersweet or ironic or melancholy  about the reality that Fidel Castro will  never see the Cuba that I believe will  soon exist which will be so different  from the Cuba that existed during his  life what's the future of Cuba I mean  Raul Castro himself is 85 years old at  this time and it was suggested that the  passing of Fidel uh might totally open  the door to real systemic change in Cuba  uh we saw much of that change before his  death but we may not see more until Raul  Castro is is no longer in power uh more  broadly Castro was one of the last sort  of major players of the Cold War  Kissinger is still around and talking to  Donald Trump and we'll we'll deal with  that probably in the next week or two  some of the Khmer Rouge are still around  but but largely inconsequential at this  point the death of Fidel Castro is not  only a story about Cuba and about Castro  and about revolution but it's also a  story about President Obama and as I  mentioned before and I want to touch on  this a little more it's also a story  about Venezuela which for a long time  was supplying so much stuff to Cuba but  Venezuela is in really rough shape right  now whacking toilet paper lacking salt  all sorts of just basics lacking in  Venezuela and